WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.490
<v ->So finally, here is a quick overview</v>

2
00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.380
of the different services you can use

3
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.130
on AWS to help you with your on-premise migration.

4
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.160
So the first one is Application Discovery Services

5
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.140
and it will help you plan migration projects

6
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.140
by gathering information about on-premise data centers.

7
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.430
It will track some stuff,

8
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.470
such as the server utilization data

9
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:23.430
and also map the dependencies before you do your migration.

10
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.320
So there's two kind of discoveries

11
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.300
for Application Discovery Services.

12
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.910
There is the agentless discovery with something

13
00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.840
called the Application Discovery Agentless Connector.

14
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.900
And this is an OVA package that you have to deploy

15
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.150
onto a VMware host on-premise.

16
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.820
And this package will do some inventory

17
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.850
on all the virtual missions you have, their configuration

18
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.430
and their performance history such as CPU,

19
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.200
memory and disc usage.

20
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.160
It is going to work for any kind of operating system.

21
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.140
But there's a second way of doing things,

22
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.510
which is to use the agent-based discovery,

23
00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.340
which is really, really interesting.

24
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.830
This will gather system and configuration,

25
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.740
system performance, running processes and details

26
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.710
of all the network connection between the systems

27
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.490
by deploying an agent onto a Microsoft server,

28
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.080
an Amazon Linux, Ubuntu, RedHat, CentOS, SUSE.

29
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.020
So the idea is that this agent will literally look

30
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.200
into all the processes and also look into the network data

31
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.170
and we'll map it with all the other agents we've deployed

32
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.810
on all the other servers and this will allow us

33
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.290
to get a full map of how the servers are communicating

34
00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.540
with one another and this will allow us to discover

35
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.870
that, yes indeed, some servers seem to talk to one another

36
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.160
but we didn't know about it.

37
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.560
So this is to discover your entire mapping

38
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.370
of services, applications, and servers overall.

39
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.250
All the resulting data can be exported as a CSV

40
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.190
or viewed within something called the AWS Migration Hub

41
00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.430
and you can also query it using Amazon Athena.

42
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.430
So I just wanna do a quick Zoom

43
00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.460
on the Migration Hub data exploration.

44
00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.450
So once you collect your data from your on-premises servers,

45
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.540
you can use Athena to analyze the data collected.

46
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.180
So because the data is automatically collected

47
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.230
with the agent and it's stored in Amazon S3 buckets

48
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.270
at regular intervals.

49
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.460
So then from Athena, you can use predefined

50
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.250
or custom queries to analyze your data.

51
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.741
For example, you can figure out the types of processes

52
00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:25.320
running on each server.

53
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.390
On top of it, you have the ability

54
00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:31.110
to upload additional data sources such as, for example,

55
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.230
a CMDB export, so CMDB means

56
00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.660
Configuration Management Database.

57
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.780
So you can put all of this and use Athena

58
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.350
to do your data exploration on the Migration Hub.

59
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.990
On top of it, if you wanted to visualize this data,

60
00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.263
you can of course integrate Athena with QuickSight.

61
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.120
So now let's talk about

62
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.630
the AWS Application Migration Service or MGN.

63
00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.720
So it's an evolution of another service that AWS had bought

64
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.380
in the past, which was called CloudEndure Migration.

65
00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.540
And AWS used to have this service called

66
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.640
Server Migration Service, SMS.

67
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.250
Now, both of them combined together are equal

68
00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.260
to the Application Migration Service

69
00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.170
and if you see CloudEndure Migration or SMS

70
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.050
in your exam, this is the exact same service.

71
00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.140
All right, so the purpose of the MGN service

72
00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.960
is to do lift and shift, so re-host solution

73
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.050
to simplify migrating applications to AWS.

74
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.060
What it'll do is that it will convert your physical,

75
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.200
virtual or cloud-based servers to run natively on AWS.

76
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.720
So as an example, you have your corporate data center,

77
00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.460
you have your OS, your apps, your database,

78
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.020
your discs and you wanna move them

79
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.870
into the cloud using the MGN service.

80
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.970
For this, you're going to create a staging environment

81
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.270
and then you will run the replication agent from AWS

82
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.980
and the replication user will continuously

83
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.890
replicate the data, your applications and so on,

84
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.620
onto your staging environment, which will correspond

85
00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.590
to an EC2 instance and EBS volumes.

86
00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.480
And then when you're ready to fully migrate,

87
00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.570
you can do a cut-over into a production environment

88
00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:04.680
to have a largely side EC2 instance and EBS volumes.

89
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.860
So this service, the Application Migration Service, MGN,

90
00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.570
supports a right range of platforms,

91
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.327
supports different operating systems, as well as databases,

92
00:04:12.327 --> 00:04:15.363
and it gives you minimal downtime and reduced costs.

93
00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.960
And so the second service that looks a lot like MGN

94
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.080
is called Elastic Disaster Recovery or DRS.

95
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.298
And why, well, it used to be called

96
00:04:24.298 --> 00:04:26.340
CloudEndure Disaster Recovery,

97
00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.420
but it's been renamed and rebranded as a pure AWS service.

98
00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.820
So the way it works, with this service,

99
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.700
you can quickly and easily recover your physical,

100
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.860
virtual or cloud-based servers into AWS,

101
00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.720
which is very similar from the previous one,

102
00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.640
but this one is about recovery.

103
00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.050
So for example, you want to protect

104
00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.293
your most critical databases, for example, Oracle, MySQL

105
00:04:45.293 --> 00:04:48.330
and SQL Server, you wanna protect your enterprise apps,

106
00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.940
for example, SAP, or you wanna protect yourself

107
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.770
from ransomware attacks and so on.

108
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.930
And so for this, you're going to get continuous,

109
00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.140
block-level replication for your servers into the cloud.

110
00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.760
So the architecture is very similar

111
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.400
because they actually rely on the same backend technology.

112
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.050
So you have your OS, your apps, your database

113
00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.970
and your discs that are going to be pushed

114
00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.150
into the cloud using the Elastic Disaster Recovery.

115
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.330
So you need to have a replication agent that will replicate

116
00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.580
within seconds all your data into the cloud.

117
00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.090
And then if you are to do a disaster recovery,

118
00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.070
then you can perform a failover within minutes

119
00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.967
to get a full-blown production environment ready

120
00:05:22.967 --> 00:05:26.040
in AWS in case, for example, your corporate data center

121
00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.400
goes down and in case you want to failover back,

122
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.380
then you can have a failback mechanism

123
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.963
when your corporate data center is available again.

124
00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.860
So to summarize, there is a few services you can use

125
00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.680
with on-premises, so first of all, just so you know,

126
00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.620
you can download the Amazon Linux 2 AMI as a virtual machine

127
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.020
in the ISO format, which allows you to load it

128
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.980
onto VMware, KVM, VirtualBox and Microsoft Hyper-V.

129
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.420
Then when it comes to moving from on-premises to the cloud,

130
00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.730
you can use the AWS Application Discovery Service

131
00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:57.780
to gather together information about

132
00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.180
your on-premises servers and plan migration.

133
00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.460
You're going to get information about server utilization

134
00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.650
and dependency mapping and then you can track

135
00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:08.370
the entire migration with the AWS Migration Hub.

136
00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:12.480
Then you have the AWS Application Migration Service or MGN,

137
00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.700
which is replacing the SMS service

138
00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.890
or the CloudEndure Migration Service.

139
00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.020
And what you're going to get is incremental replication

140
00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.290
of on-premises live servers into AWS.

141
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:26.010
You can migrate the entire VM into AWS with this service.

142
00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.520
Then you have the DRS service, so Elastic Disaster Recovery,

143
00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.800
which is very similar to MGN, but this one

144
00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.220
is replacing what's called CloudEndure Disaster Recovery.

145
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.650
And it's going to help you recover on-premises workload

146
00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.590
onto AWS through a disaster recovery strategy.

147
00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.180
And then finally, not mentioning in this lecture

148
00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:43.650
but you've known about it already,

149
00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.440
this is the AWS Database Migration Service or DMS,

150
00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:47.820
which is going to be used to replicate

151
00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.330
your on-premises servers onto AWS or from AWS to AWS

152
00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.450
or even from AWS to on-premises

153
00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.310
and this is for databases only, of course.

154
00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:59.250
It works for Oracle, MySQL DB, DynamoDB,

155
00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.630
et cetera, et cetera.

156
00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:02.250
All right, so that's it for this lecture.

157
00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.250
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

